---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageMeta from '@/components/PageMeta.astro';
import Diagram from '@/components/Diagram.astro';
---

<Layout title="Validation, review and projections" description="How SPDTF separates open-world ontology meaning, SHACL checks, conformance evidence, human semantic review, governance and generated projections.">
  <PageMeta category="How we model SPDTF" variant="brand" updated="Validation scope · technical success is not semantic approval" />
  <h1>Validation, review and generated projections</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Validation makes encoded expectations testable. Conformance states which exact
    features and probes were exercised. Neither one approves meaning: practitioners
    review the candidate and recorded governance controls any promotion.
  </p>

  <h2 id="open-closed">Open-world meaning and closed-world checks</h2>
  <p>
    RDF/RDFS/OWL use an open-world model: absence of a statement does not normally make
    the statement false. This is useful when knowledge arrives from many sources. SHACL
    applies a declared validation lens to a supplied data graph: for a particular
    delivery, it can require a value, datatype, class, count or pattern.
  </p>
  <Diagram size="large" caption="One data graph participates in two different regimes. RDFS and OWL describe semantic implications in an open world. A separate SHACL shapes graph and the data graph enter a validator together. Neither branch establishes factual truth.">
flowchart LR
  accTitle: Open-world ontology meaning compared with SHACL validation
  accDescr: A supplied RDF data graph feeds an ontology semantics branch; separately, that data graph and a SHACL shapes graph both enter a validator which creates a report. Both branches produce bounded evidence rather than business approval.
  Data["Supplied RDF data graph"]:::data
  Meaning["RDFS and OWL semantics"]:::service
  Inference["Declared implications"]:::infra
  Shapes["SHACL shapes graph"]:::security
  Validator["SHACL validator"]:::process
  Report["Validation report"]:::process
  Truth["No factual truth or approval claim"]:::warning
  Data --> Meaning
  Meaning --> Inference
  Data --> Validator
  Shapes --> Validator
  Validator --> Report
  Inference -.-> Truth
  Report -.-> Truth
  </Diagram>
  <p>
    RDFS domain and range are semantic statements and may infer types; they are not
    mandatory-form fields. SHACL property shapes encode delivery expectations. Copying
    one mechanically into the other can change meaning and is not allowed without a
    reviewed rule.
  </p>

  <h2 id="automated">What automated checks can establish</h2>
  <table>
    <caption>Different checks answer different questions</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Check</th><th scope="col">Can establish</th><th scope="col">Cannot establish</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Parser</th><td>Files use supported syntax and can be loaded.</td><td>Terms express correct or useful meaning.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Graph invariants</th><td>Identifiers, imports, provenance fields and package rules meet declared tests.</td><td>The source evidence is true or authoritative.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">SHACL</th><td>Jena reports whether the supplied fixture conforms to the supplied shapes graph.</td><td>Every emitted feature is independently covered, or the business rule is universally correct.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Competency query</th><td>The candidate can answer the encoded structural question on test data.</td><td>The answer represents practitioner consensus.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Projection comparison</th><td>A generated output preserves declared structures and examples.</td><td>Every consumer will interpret it correctly.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Property Pack 0.1 runs its complete shapes graph against one conforming fixture and
    one deliberately non-conforming fixture. That proves the recorded end-to-end cases,
    not a positive and negative probe for every emitted SHACL feature. Missing tools do
    fail the current run. Rejecting unrecognised vocabulary and incomplete feature
    receipts is an intended future gate; the current validator does not yet implement
    that allow-list, so it must not be claimed as evidence.
  </p>

  <h2 id="conformance">Bounded conformance claims</h2>
  <p>
    Property Pack 0.1 declares RDF 1.2 Basic-compatible Turtle, a bounded RDFS/OWL
    vocabulary surface, SKOS, an exercised SHACL 1.2 Core surface and an ASK/basic-graph-pattern
    design subset tested in ARQ 6.1.0. It does not claim cross-engine SPARQL portability,
    full RDF 1.2, OWL-profile reasoning,
    SHACL Union, SHACL-AF, SHACL-SPARQL or general SPARQL 1.2 conformance.
  </p>
  <p>
    Property Pack 0.1 records specification snapshots, engine versions, a test corpus,
    results and known nonclaims. It does not yet publish a complete machine-readable
    inventory of every enabled RDFS, OWL, SKOS, XSD and SHACL feature. That inventory is
    a requirement for a stronger conformance claim. Updating an evolving Working Draft
    is a governed change that reruns the probes; a page must not silently relabel an
    older pinned snapshot as “latest”.
  </p>

  <h2 id="authority">Semantic review and governance promotion</h2>
  <Diagram size="large" caption="Technical evidence accumulates before semantic review. Practitioners decide whether the meaning fits their domain; a separately authorised governance record controls promotion. Proposed lifecycle stages remain visibly proposed.">
flowchart LR
  accTitle: Technical checks semantic review and governance authority
  accDescr: Syntax, graph, SHACL and competency checks produce technical evidence; practitioners review meaning; governance may record a lifecycle decision but automated tools cannot promote the candidate.
  Syntax["Syntax and graph checks"]:::data
  Constraints["SHACL and query checks"]:::process
  Evidence["Bounded technical evidence"]:::info
  Review["Practitioner semantic review"]:::user
  Decision["Authorised governance record"]:::security
  Release["Versioned candidate or release"]:::service
  Syntax --> Evidence
  Constraints --> Evidence
  Evidence --> Review
  Review --> Decision
  Decision --> Release
  Review -.->|revise or seek evidence| Constraints
  </Diagram>
  <p>
    The current SPDTF-wide lifecycle described in ADR-0068 remains <strong>Proposed</strong>.
    Outside the separately accepted accelerated Property Pack sequence, do not label a
    candidate as adopted, ratified or released unless a later accepted decision names the
    authority and records the act.
  </p>

  <h2 id="projections">Generated projections</h2>
  <p>
    A governed semantic package may generate RDF/OWL/SKOS, SHACL constraints, JSON
    Schema, JSON-LD contexts, forms, APIs, website pages, PDF and Markdown. Each projection
    names its source package, generator, transformation boundary and tests. A downstream
    edit cannot silently redefine the maintained model.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Projection assurance questions</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Required evidence</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Which package produced it?</th><td>Immutable package identifier and content digest.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Which information can the target represent?</th><td>Explicit loss, flattening and unsupported-feature statement.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">How was it generated?</th><td>Versioned deterministic generator and configuration.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">How was it checked?</th><td>Positive/negative fixtures, round-trip or semantic-diff receipt where appropriate.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Who reviewed its fitness?</th><td>Named consumer/use case and human review record.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <a href="/spdtf/ontologies">Return to semantic modelling</a> ·
    <a href="/spdtf/property-pack/validation">Inspect Property Pack validation evidence</a>
  </p>
</Layout>
